window.CARDS=window.CARDS||{};CARDS["school-system-private-points"]=[{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p30-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points initial point grant   core economic rules complete institutional isolation  anhs is an enclosed boarding campus  students are prohibited from leaving the grounds or contacting outside individuals  including family and friends  without administrative permission  point currency parity  1 private point   1 japanese yen  1 pt    1  all on campus transactions  cafeteria  convenience stores  mall  equipment  services  are conducted exclusively with points via digital mobile terminals  monthly point grant  homeroom teacher chabashira announces that 100 000 points are deposited into each student s account on the first of every month  ostensibly reflecting their merit and evaluation for passing the entrance exam  transferability   anti extortion  points may be freely transferred between students via terminals  coercion  extortion  and theft of points are strictly prohibited and monitored by school authorities  non convertible   graduation expiration  points cannot be converted into real world cash  and all unspent points revert to the school upon graduation  dormitory utility policy  dormitories  single rooms  keycard access  do not charge or deduct points for water  gas  or electricity consumption  deliberate administrative trap  the school deliberately conceals the connection between student conduct  class evaluation  class points  and monthly deposits  allowing class d to foolishly assume 100 000 points is guaranteed free money","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p30-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Initial point grant &amp; core economic rules<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.30–32<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Detail<\/span> Deliberate administrative trap: the school deliberately conceals the connection between student conduct, class evaluation (Class Points), and monthly deposits, allowing Class D to foolishly assume 100,000 points is guaranteed free money.<span class=\"pg\">p.32<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p106-1\">→ After: Free survival supplies &amp; safety valve mechanism<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p106-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points free survival supplies   safety valve mechanism the zero point safety valve  the school store features a dedicated lower shelf section of  entirely free goods  free basic sanitary items  toilet paper  notebooks  pencils  and emergency food rations  allocation rule  students are permitted to take up to one package per month free of charge  institutional deduction  the school designed this infrastructure from inception anticipating that certain students would exhaust or lose all their private points  ensuring basic survival without cash or points","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p106-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Free survival supplies &amp; safety valve mechanism<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.106–109<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p30-1\">← Before: Initial point grant &amp; core economic rules<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p120-1\">→ After: The S-System unmasked: Class Points, zero-point baseline, &amp; Class A monopoly<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p120-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points the s system unmasked  class points  zero point baseline    class a monopoly the s system evaluation deduction  the monthly deposit is directly tied to the collective evaluation of the class  on may 1st  class d receives  0 private points  due to april disciplinary infractions   98 absences and late arrivals   391 incidences of talking or mobile phone usage in class  total penalty completely wipes out the baseline 100 000 point monthly allowance  initial grade wide class point standings  may 1st  2015   class a  940 points  94 000 private points student   class b  650 points  65 000 private points student   class c  490 points  49 000 private points student   class d  0 points  0 private points student  proprietary evaluation secrecy  the school refuses to disclose the exact formula or weighted rubric used for point deductions and rewards  comparing it to corporate employee evaluations  the zero point trap  the ratchet baseline  stopping negative behavior  tardiness  talking  merely halts further deductions  it does  not  grant positive points  once a class hits 0 points  it remains at 0 points the next month unless affirmative merit points are actively earned  meritocratic class sorting  students are sorted upon entrance into four tiers based on overall competence  class a represents the most capable elite  class d is the bottom depository for failures  chabashira   you re defective  this is just the result of you being defective  dynamic class promotion  class letters are not fixed  if class d accumulates more points than class c  class d immediately becomes class c and vice versa  zero point survival guarantee  students with 0 points do not starve  housing in dormitories and basic food rations remain guaranteed by the school  red mark instant expulsion  receiving a single failing score  red mark  on any midterm or final examination results in  immediate  irreversible expulsion  from the school  class a monopoly on privilege  the school s renowned guarantee of  100  employment and college matriculation  applies  strictly and exclusively to graduates of class a  classes b  c  and d receive no placement assistance from the school and are left entirely to their own devices  terminal point transfer mechanism  private points can be transferred bilaterally between students directly through their school issued smartphones by entering the recipient s phone number","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p120-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">The S-System unmasked: Class Points, zero-point baseline, &amp; Class A monopoly<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.120–130, 138–139<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p106-1\">← Before: Free survival supplies &amp; safety valve mechanism<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p152-1\">→ After: Midterm pass threshold &amp; expulsion-avoidance economics<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p152-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points midterm pass threshold   expulsion avoidance economics midterm pass threshold  the exact pass line is  32 points    scoring at least 32 is safe  anything below is a red mark   immediate expulsion  sudou asks for the exact cutoff  ike confirms it to him  high marks feed class assessment  per hirata s announcement  strong midterm scores should improve the class s overall evaluation  on top of preventing expulsion  study group scoring policy  horikita sets the target at  50 points for everyone  refusing to coach them to merely skate by at the 32 point threshold  aiming to just skate by is dangerous  the dead weight calculus  expelled failures raise the class average test scores without further effort  horikita argues that dumping the failures leaves only the better students  making a higher class rank simpler to attain   the position she defends to ayanokoji in their night debate and concedes is  basically correct  only after he proves her blind to others  unique strengths","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p152-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Midterm pass threshold &amp; expulsion-avoidance economics<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.152, 173–174, 178, 197–199<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p120-1\">← Before: The S-System unmasked: Class Points, zero-point baseline, &amp; Class A monopoly<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p229-1\">→ After: The old-test gray market<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p229-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points the old test gray market past exam papers are openly tradeable  no school rule bars studying from old test problems  point transfers are logged but legal  and barter among students  even across years  is routine enough that a third year negotiates without surprise  ayanokoji buys a third year class d student s first year midterm answer sheet  plus the post admission mock answers as a bonus  opening offer 10 000   seller asks 30 000   ak shows 20 000   settles at  15 000 points  transferred in advance  transfer logs serve as mutual anti fraud insurance  zero point students are the natural sellers  excluded from cafes  they eat the  free vegetable meal set  making cash points the ideal lever  ak targets his stakeout specifically by watching who buys the free set  old test value model  identical mock papers across years prove the test bank repeats verbatim   old papers are  insurance  not a guarantee for the midterm  distribution strategy  withhold the papers from the trio until the day before the test  then release online to trigger an overnight class wide cram  kushida fronts the story  papers  from a third year student you re close to  so ayanokoji stays invisible","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p229-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">The old-test gray market<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.229–233<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p152-1\">← Before: Midterm pass threshold &amp; expulsion-avoidance economics<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p253-1\">→ After: Buying an exam point (the 100,000-point sale)<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p253-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points buying an exam point  the 100 000 point sale  test points are purchasable in principle  on admission students were told they can buy anything with points  and  the midterm test is just one more  thing  at this school    chabashira neither denies the mechanism nor cites any rule against it  price discovery for a single english test point   100 000 points   the exceptional price  for a first ever request  ayanokoji and horikita each contribute via student id transfer   combined 100 000 points   and sudou s failing mark is re scored to a pass on the spot  expulsion cancelled  practical scarcity  no student retains a spare 100 000 points weeks into the term   only a two payer coalition can afford the transaction  the sale is half comic but fully binding  chabashira takes both ids  collects  and treats the result as official  there s nothing more to be done","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p253-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Buying an exam point (the 100,000-point sale)<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.253–254<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The sale is half-comic but fully binding: Chabashira takes both IDs, collects, and treats the result as official (&quot;There&#x27;s nothing more to be done.&quot;).<span class=\"pg\">p.253–254<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p229-1\">← Before: The old-test gray market<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p257-1\">→ After: No points for the worst class<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v1-p257-1","v":"y1v1","y":1,"o":1,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points no points for the worst class class d carries the  lowest average score of all four classes by far  after the midterm   horikita s verdict  no point allocation next month either  ground level poverty  after spending their points  ike and yamauchi live off the school s free offerings   the zero balance survival mode the warning track promised  default expectation set in voice  don t count on monthly points in class d   learn to live a more modest life and give up on points   horikita  the class s first explicit long game framing  point acquisition   exam performance are separate axes    studying isn t the sole factor in deciding who goes into what class","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p257-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">No points for the worst class<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V1 · p.257–258<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The class&#x27;s first explicit long-game framing: point acquisition + exam performance are separate axes — &quot;Studying isn&#x27;t the sole factor in deciding who goes into what class.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.258<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v1-p253-1\">← Before: Buying an exam point (the 100,000-point sale)<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p15-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points july 1  the app mechanics  first payout  delayed deposit school app functions enumerated  balance inquiry  personal balance  collective class points  and point transfer to another student  point types suffixed  cl   class  and  pr   private   on the first day of every month  they multiplied the cl points  or class points  by 100  then deposited that amount into our private point accounts  zero class points   zero spending money  real currency is refused anywhere on campus  free cafeteria meals are the floor for broke students  every class admitted with 1000 class points  class d bled to 0 by may and stayed 0 through june  july 1 board  class a 1004  slightly above admission baseline  classes b c each  100 over june  class d 87   first nonzero total  interpretive precedent  horikita  a uniform  100 jump for all first year classes reads as the midterm completion reward  not merit  relative rank  not raw points  drives promotion  deposits can be delayed institution wide   the first year students  point distribution has been delayed  chabashira s rider   once the trouble has been resolved  you ll receive your points  if there are still points left  that is  school supermarket offers free ingredients  same subsidy logic as the free cafeteria set    horikita s bento runs on them  disciplinary economics  first instance  an unresolved inter class fight threatens both an individual summer suspension for sudou and a class wide point deduction  the school granted a proof window until  next tuesday  before ruling fault  sample drain rate  ayanokoji s post midterm point purchase balance 8 320 private points  87 class points would have paid  8700 yen per student","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p15-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">July 1: the app mechanics, first payout, delayed deposit<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.15–20<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Every class admitted with 1000 class points; Class D bled to 0 by May and stayed 0 through June.<span class=\"pg\">p.16<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> July 1 board: Class A 1004 (slightly above admission baseline); Classes B/C each ~+100 over June; Class D 87 — first nonzero total.<span class=\"pg\">p.17<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Sample drain rate: Ayanokōji&#x27;s post-midterm-point-purchase balance 8,320 private points; 87 class points would have paid ~8700 yen per student.<span class=\"pg\">p.27<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p40-1\">→ After: Ch.3 sec. 3.1: what a disciplinary case does to a class&#x27;s balance<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p40-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 3 sec  3 1  what a disciplinary case does to a class s balance the two headed cost of a fight  stated by the school   depending on the degree of responsibility that class c saddled him with  sudou could face suspension  and we might see a reduction in our class points  individual punishment and class punishment are separate dials turned by the same incident  punishment is deferred against evidence  not against pleas   we ll make our final judgment next tuesday  taking into account any eyewitnesses and evidence  a witness is worth a stay of execution  the search is institutionally widened  which is itself a cost   in order to check for witnesses  every teacher will inform their classes of the details of this incident    the incident s reputation is now school wide property  class d s own arithmetic on the risk  from ike   if we lose points because of sudou  will we be at zero again this month    with 87 class points deposited  p 17  any deduction returns the class to zero private income  horikita states the recovery model out loud   for class d to rise through the ranks  it s important to recover the class points we lost as quickly as possible  however  we probably won t get any points now  thanks to you    a deduction is priced not as the points lost but as the month s income forfeited  the argument she refuses to accept is moral  not economic   you keep focusing on who started the fight  but that s a trivial detail    under this system the school s liability finding  not the truth of who swung first  sets the deduction  a witness is only worth what their class position allows   even if there had been a witness  it d be checkmate for us if that witness happened to come from class c  it would be natural for a class c student to hide the truth in order to protect his or her fellow classmates  this school was a hierarchy  after all  class interest predicts who will help  ak opens with class b because it is  the class that would most want a witness     between d and c  which class most threatens class b    and skips class a on the grounds that  i don t think they d want to get involved in a troublesome affair that might negatively impact their points  nowhere in chapter 3 is a point figure transferred  held in escrow  or offered as inducement  every lever in this chapter is the threat of a future deduction  and no student pays or receives points for the witness search","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p40-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3 sec. 3.1: what a disciplinary case does to a class&#x27;s balance<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.40–47<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Class D&#x27;s own arithmetic on the risk, from Ike: &quot;If we lose points because of Sudou, will we be at zero again this month?&quot; — with 87 class points deposited (p.17), any deduction returns the class to zero private income.<span class=\"pg\">p.41<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Nowhere in Chapter 3 is a point figure transferred, held in escrow, or offered as inducement; every lever in this chapter is the threat of a future deduction, and no student pays or receives points for the witness search.<span class=\"pg\">p.40–61<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p15-1\">← Before: July 1: the app mechanics, first payout, delayed deposit<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p68-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.0: the price of a class change, and the points nobody told Class D about<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p68-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 0  the price of a class change  and the points nobody told class d about class change is a purchase  announced by the school itself   i said that there s a way to reach class a without class points  and  i told you on the day you arrived  i said there s nothing you cannot buy with your points at this school  in other words  you can change classes by using your personal points  the price is fixed and stated as a rule  not a rumour   twenty million  do your best to conserve points  do that  and you can enter whichever class you like  why the number is that high   if i were reduce the number by one digit  there would probably be over 100 people in class a come graduation  then there d be no point to our system   sic    if i were reduce  the honest ceiling  from the school   even if you perfectly maintain your class points upon starting school here  after three years you d only have 3 6 million  class a could raise points enough to reach four million  normally  you simply wouldn t be able to do it  nobody has ever paid it   unfortunately  no  no one has done it before  the record  and how it ended   about three years ago  one student from class b saved nearly 12 million points before graduating     he was expelled before graduation  however  so he was unable to save those 20 million points in the end  he was engaged in a large scale fraudulent operation to save up points  what the fraud was  and the school s verdict on the motive   he took advantage of the newly admitted first year students who didn t yet understand the system  he went to them one by one and scammed points out of them  so he could reach the 20 million points necessary to move to class a     i don t think that his goal was particularly bad  but people who break the rules must be punished  a second  unadvertised income track exists   there are cases where individuals may win points based on their efforts in club activities  or their degree of participation  for example  if someone in a calligraphy club were to win a contest  they could receive corresponding points for that award  the information was unevenly distributed by design or by carelessness    the other classes should have already been well informed of this    and class d had not been told at all  three months in  the school s position on points driven club joining   do you really think you would ve been able to see any results after joining a club  results like winning prizes or being in games  with such shallow feelings  a remembered instance of the club track  told secondhand by hirata rather than shown  their p e  instructor higashiyama sensei had promised  the student who got the best time during our first class would get 5000 points  the scale gap is the real content of the announcement   for students in class d  who dreamed of a mere 100 or 200 private points  reaching 20 million was a faraway dream  ak and horikita are the only two who can verify the claim from experience    we had tested out that very method ourselves by buying a test point from the school    the volume 1 english point purchase is the private proof behind a school wide rule","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p68-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.0: the price of a class change, and the points nobody told Class D about<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.68–72<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>7 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The honest ceiling, from the school: &quot;Even if you perfectly maintain your class points upon starting school here, after three years you’d only have 3.6 million. Class A could raise points enough to reach four million. Normally, you simply wouldn’t be able to do it.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.69<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Nobody has ever paid it: &quot;Unfortunately, no. No one has done it before.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.69<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The record, and how it ended: &quot;About three years ago, one student from Class B saved nearly 12 million points before graduating.&quot; — &quot;He was expelled before graduation, however, so he was unable to save those 20 million points in the end. He was engaged in a large-scale fraudulent operation to save up points.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.70<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The information was unevenly distributed by design or by carelessness — &quot;The other classes should have already been well informed of this.&quot; — and Class D had not been told at all, three months in.<span class=\"pg\">p.71<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> A remembered instance of the club track, told secondhand by Hirata rather than shown: their P.E. instructor Higashiyama-sensei had promised &quot;the student who got the best time during our first class would get 5000 points.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.72<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Limit<\/span> The scale gap is the real content of the announcement: &quot;For students in Class D, who dreamed of a mere 100 or 200 private points, reaching 20 million was a faraway dream.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.70<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> AK and Horikita are the only two who can verify the claim from experience — &quot;We had tested out that very method ourselves by buying a test point from the school.&quot; — the Volume 1 English-point purchase is the private proof behind a school-wide rule.<span class=\"pg\">p.68<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p40-1\">← Before: Ch.3 sec. 3.1: what a disciplinary case does to a class&#x27;s balance<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p95-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.3–4.4: the cameras that grade you, and the tracks Class D was never told about<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p95-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 3 4 4  the cameras that grade you  and the tracks class d was never told about classrooms are recorded  and the recording is the grading instrument   the school checks those cameras to see if we talk or fall asleep during class  otherwise  they wouldn t be able to accurately assess our monthly performance    ak locates them physically   i pointed at the two or so cameras affixed to the ceiling near the corners of the classroom  they were small enough that they wouldn t be a distraction  and they blended in well with their surroundings  but they were undeniably security cameras  the cameras  evidentiary value  established the same day   well  there are cameras that record what goes on in the classrooms  right  therefore  if anything did happen  there d be evidence of it  those recordings would blow the class c guys  lies away  the coverage boundary  checked on site  the special building s hallways have no cameras    there were electrical outlets by the ceiling  but no signs they were being used    and ak generalises   it probably wasn t just the special building  the hallways in the main school buildings probably didn t have cameras  either  horikita s own limit case   if i had to think of places where they wouldn t be installed  i d probably say the bathrooms and the locker rooms  right  club performance is not a private income side track only  it feeds the class total   the school evaluates club and philanthropic activities  right  so if he enters a tournament and does well  sudou kun could earn points  those ll be tied to your class points  too  wait  did you guys not know that    spoken by a class b student  and class d had heard only the private points half of it  the staff side of the same machine  offered as explanation for a teacher s apathy   did you know that the school evaluates homeroom teachers when their class graduates    ichinose s claim  the school itself never states it in this volume  nobody in class d knew about the cameras either   seriously  i never knew that  and  they re not easily spotted  i also didn t notice until they talked about points for the first time  three system facts in one day  classroom cameras  club points reaching the class total  teacher evaluation  all arrived from a teacher s slip  a student s memory or another class   none of them from an official notice  the information gap  not the point maths  is what class d keeps paying for","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p95-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.3–4.4: the cameras that grade you, and the tracks Class D was never told about<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.95, p.107<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The coverage boundary, checked on site: the special building&#x27;s hallways have no cameras — &quot;There were electrical outlets by the ceiling, but no signs they were being used.&quot; — and AK generalises: &quot;It probably wasn’t just the special building. The hallways in the main school buildings probably didn’t have cameras, either.&quot; Horikita&#x27;s own limit case: &quot;If I had to think of places where they wouldn’t be installed, I’d probably say the bathrooms and the locker rooms. Right?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.98–99<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Limit<\/span> Nobody in Class D knew about the cameras either: &quot;Seriously?! I never knew that!&quot; and &quot;They’re not easily spotted. I also didn’t notice until they talked about points for the first time.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.95<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Three system facts in one day (classroom cameras, club points reaching the class total, teacher evaluation) all arrived from a teacher&#x27;s slip, a student&#x27;s memory or another class — none of them from an official notice. The information gap, not the point maths, is what Class D keeps paying for.<span class=\"pg\">p.95–107<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p68-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.0: the price of a class change, and the points nobody told Class D about<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p108-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.4–4.6: the teacher&#x27;s own incentive, and the roles Class D never made<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p108-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 4 4 6  the teacher s own incentive  and the roles class d never made the staff side of the class system  from a class b student   the homeroom teacher for class a gets a special bonus    reported as hoshinomiya sensei s stated motivation  and the first explanation the volume offers for why teaching quality is uneven between classes  class d s own reading of the gap  ak on chabashira    our teacher appeared to lack ambition  or even an interest in money  it felt like even if we collapsed into failure  she d just say it was great  and  other classes pitied us  if anything  this showed how little interest chabashira sensei felt for her own students  classes may invent their own governance with no school mandate  class b elected a class representative  a vice representative and a secretary  purely informal   something class b set up on its own    and the roles are justified as machinery for the cultural fair and sports festival  class d has none   i understood her point  but we certainly weren t going to assign our own class representative    the volume s explanation of why class d organises through four individuals  hirata  kushida  horikita  ayanokoji  instead of through offices  neither fact is confirmed by the school  both arrive as secondhand report from class b  the bonus claim is the volume s only stated material incentive attached to a teacher  and it is never repeated by a teacher","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p108-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.4–4.6: the teacher&#x27;s own incentive, and the roles Class D never made<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.108, p.114–115<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>3 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Class D&#x27;s own reading of the gap: AK on Chabashira — &quot;Our teacher appeared to lack ambition, or even an interest in money. It felt like even if we collapsed into failure, she’d just say it was great.&quot; and &quot;Other classes pitied us. If anything, this showed how little interest Chabashira-sensei felt for her own students.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.108<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Class D has none: &quot;I understood her point, but we certainly weren’t going to assign our own class representative.&quot; — the volume&#x27;s explanation of why Class D organises through four individuals (Hirata, Kushida, Horikita, Ayanokōji) instead of through offices.<span class=\"pg\">p.115<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Neither fact is confirmed by the school; both arrive as secondhand report from Class B. The bonus claim is the volume&#x27;s only stated material incentive attached to a teacher, and it is never repeated by a teacher.<span class=\"pg\">p.108, p.115<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p95-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.3–4.4: the cameras that grade you, and the tracks Class D was never told about<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p116-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.6: the first figures any student says out loud<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p116-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"inner spoken","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 6  the first figures any student says out loud  i ve heard rumors that we might be taking a vacation to a tropical island    the summer reward exists in july only as a rumour a class b student repeats  the school has announced nothing  the connection back to class d s own teacher   that sparked a memory  i d forgotten about it  but chabashira sensei had mentioned a vacation  what class b expects the break to be worth   i think there might be a hidden task  one that has a bigger impact on us than the midterms or final exams  otherwise  the difference between class a and us wouldn t close much  we could diminish the gap little by little  class b s standing  given freely   we have about 600 points  so our classes are around 350 points apart   so far  the midterm is all that s given us a chance to raise our class points  so losing at least a few points was unavoidable for us  i mean  even class a has lost points    the first statement in the volume that a drop in class points is normal rather than punishment  class d s actual month   class d had only managed to get 87 points this month  we weren t anywhere close to being able to compete with the others  the narrator s own sizing of the coming event   it would probably be worth more than 10 or 20 points  however  it was difficult to imagine that it would change things by 500 or even 1 000 points  every figure in this stretch is a student s report  not a school announcement  and the only two classes whose numbers are compared are b and a  class c is absent from the account entirely","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p116-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.6: the first figures any student says out loud<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.116–118<\/span><\/h3><ul class=\"lines\"><li class=\"q spoken\">&quot;I’ve heard rumors that we might be taking a vacation to a tropical island.&quot; — the summer reward exists in July only as a rumour a Class B student repeats; the school has announced nothing.<span class=\"pg\">p.116<\/span><\/li><li class=\"q inner\">The connection back to Class D&#x27;s own teacher: &quot;That sparked a memory. I’d forgotten about it, but Chabashira-sensei had mentioned a vacation.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.116<\/span><\/li><\/ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>4 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Class B&#x27;s standing, given freely: &quot;We have about 600 points, so our classes are around 350 points apart.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.117<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> &quot;So far, the midterm is all that’s given us a chance to raise our class points, so losing at least a few points was unavoidable for us. I mean, even Class A has lost points.&quot; — the first statement in the volume that a drop in class points is normal rather than punishment.<span class=\"pg\">p.117<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Class D&#x27;s actual month: &quot;Class D had only managed to get 87 points this month. We weren’t anywhere close to being able to compete with the others.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.117<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Every figure in this stretch is a student&#x27;s report, not a school announcement, and the only two classes whose numbers are compared are B and A; Class C is absent from the account entirely.<span class=\"pg\">p.117–118<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p108-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.4–4.6: the teacher&#x27;s own incentive, and the roles Class D never made<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p138-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.10: the shops licensed to the school, and what a warranty is worth<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p138-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 10  the shops licensed to the school  and what a warranty is worth  there were some incredibly famous  nationally known stores that did business with our school  even though their customers were only students and the stores themselves weren t too very big  they sold items for everyday use and electronic appliances  the camera s fault  and its cure   when the store clerk opened up the camera to confirm the contents  he saw that part of it had been damaged from the fall  that was why the camera wouldn t turn on properly  fortunately  because sakura still had the warranty card  the item could be repaired free of charge  the wait  the repair is quoted at two weeks  and chapter 4 ends long before the camera comes back  a student s own account of consumer law  used to shield a classmate from handing over her address   the manufacturer warranty explicitly shows where the item was sold and the date of purchase  also  i doubt there d be any legal problems with me putting my information down  it should be perfectly fine if the user s name is different than the purchaser s  the store accepts it without argument   n no  not at all  i understand  there s nothing to worry about  the clerk said","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p138-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.10: the shops licensed to the school, and what a warranty is worth<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.138–140<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The wait: the repair is quoted at two weeks, and Chapter 4 ends long before the camera comes back.<span class=\"pg\">p.140<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Feat<\/span> The store accepts it without argument: &quot;N-no, not at all. I understand. There’s nothing to worry about,&quot; the clerk said.<span class=\"pg\">p.140<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p116-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.6: the first figures any student says out loud<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p143-1\">→ After: Ch.4 sec. 4.10: a price a student actually paid<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p143-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4 sec  4 10  a price a student actually paid the one hard price named by a student in the volume  in the buyer s own words   yes  i most certainly did  it cost 80 000 points  but what of it  that points can be spent on electronics at all is established only by this purchase and by the shopping trip it prompts  no rule about what may be bought  or from which pool  is stated  the narrator s ceiling  stated to the two girls waiting beside him   today was just a preliminary inspection  i don t have enough points to buy anything  the contact is stored in his phone as  sotomura  professor    students trade points and expertise with each other faster than the school explains either  the thing he is price checking is not named in chapter 4  the trip is a preliminary inspection and the purchase is postponed for lack of points","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p143-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4 sec. 4.10: a price a student actually paid<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.143–144<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>4 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The one hard price named by a student in the volume, in the buyer&#x27;s own words: &quot;Yes, I most certainly did. It cost 80,000 points. But what of it?&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.143<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The narrator&#x27;s ceiling, stated to the two girls waiting beside him: &quot;Today was just a preliminary inspection. I don’t have enough points to buy anything.&quot;<span class=\"pg\">p.144<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Detail<\/span> The contact is stored in his phone as &quot;Sotomura (Professor)&quot; — students trade points and expertise with each other faster than the school explains either.<span class=\"pg\">p.143<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The thing he is price-checking is not named in Chapter 4; the trip is a preliminary inspection and the purchase is postponed for lack of points.<span class=\"pg\">p.143–144<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p138-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.10: the shops licensed to the school, and what a warranty is worth<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p152-1\">→ After: Ch.5 sec. 5.0: paying informants, and paying them anonymously<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p152-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 5 sec  5 0  paying informants  and paying them anonymously a bulletin board flyer promises points for information about sudou and class c  it is the first time in the volume that students offer their own points as a bounty  a school homepage message board is used to canvass witnesses  the view counter is  still only in the dozens  the bounty is class b s own money  not the school s   we just decided to do that on our own  anonymous payouts run on a temporary token key   from your id number  you can issue a temporary token key  if you open that  and send the key  you should get a request for payment  the points screen shows the user s id number in the upper left corner  the mechanism is demonstrated rather than explained  ayanokoji walks ichinose through the remittance screen step by step","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p152-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.5 sec. 5.0: paying informants, and paying them anonymously<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.152–158<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The mechanism is demonstrated rather than explained: Ayanokōji walks Ichinose through the remittance screen step by step.<span class=\"pg\">p.157<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p143-1\">← Before: Ch.4 sec. 4.10: a price a student actually paid<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p234-1\">→ After: Ch.7: loans, fees, and the cost of a withdrawn case<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v2-p234-1","v":"y1v2","y":1,"o":2,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 7  loans  fees  and the cost of a withdrawn case closing a withdrawn case still costs   we will require a certain payment of points in order to cover of miscellaneous expenses incurred over the course of deliberations    announced in the hearing with no speaker attached  and the first time any student has heard of it  ayanokoji lends ichinose the points for the camera purchase  she will return them   i ll definitely return the points i borrowed  just tell me when you want them    he sets the deadline at graduation","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p234-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7: loans, fees, and the cost of a withdrawn case<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V2 · p.234–245<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> Ayanokōji lends Ichinose the points for the camera purchase; she will return them: &quot;I’ll definitely return the points I borrowed. Just tell me when you want them.&quot; — he sets the deadline at graduation.<span class=\"pg\">p.234–235<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v2-p152-1\">← Before: Ch.5 sec. 5.0: paying informants, and paying them anonymously<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p11-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 2  a transfer between classmates a balance check reads 106 219 points  100 000 of them move to sudou ken in the same beat  before he calls  the page states no purpose for the transfer   the call that follows is about a fortune teller  not the money","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p11-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2: a transfer between classmates<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.11<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> A balance check reads 106,219 points; 100,000 of them move to Sudou Ken in the same beat, before he calls.<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Caveat<\/span> The page states no purpose for the transfer — the call that follows is about a fortune-teller, not the money.<span class=\"pg\">p.11<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p34-1\">→ After: Ch.2: paying for the Basic Plan<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p34-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 2  paying for the basic plan the menu s floor   we d be spending well over 5 000 points  his balance check finds roughly 6 000 left    barely enough    and both take the basic plan  the card reader deducts  price not printed","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p34-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2: paying for the Basic Plan<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.34<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> The menu’s floor: “We’d be spending well over 5,000 points.”<span class=\"pg\">p.34<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> His balance check finds roughly 6,000 left — “barely enough” — and both take the Basic Plan; the card reader deducts, price not printed.<span class=\"pg\">p.34<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p11-1\">← Before: Ch.2: a transfer between classmates<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p49-1\">→ After: Ch.3: broke until September<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p49-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 3  broke until september promised special exam points will not be deposited until september 1  ak has almost nothing left after the fortune teller spend   we had been awarded 8 700 private points at the start of august  free meal plan and free water make going a full month without spending possible  most students still run dry near month s end  first month s 100 000 points set the comparison  the boys pool 500 points for one gift","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p49-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: broke until September<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.49–50<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> “We had been awarded 8,700 private points at the start of August.”<span class=\"pg\">p.50<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> First month&#x27;s 100,000 points set the comparison; the boys pool 500 points for one gift.<span class=\"pg\">p.50<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p34-1\">← Before: Ch.2: paying for the Basic Plan<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p67-1\">→ After: Ch.3: summer clothes priced out<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p67-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 3  summer clothes priced out summer clothes sell at rather high prices  some class d girls wanted them but had to do without","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p67-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: summer clothes priced out<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.67<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p49-1\">← Before: Ch.3: broke until September<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p82-1\">→ After: Ch.3: the reward economy<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p82-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 3  the reward economy ak is asked for about 1 500 points toward katsuragi s present  his expenses double  katsuragi offers sudou 100 000 points for the delivery   next to sudou s daily 1 000 2 000  20 000 paid as advance  80 000 on completion  proving complicity to both parties  payment routes through ak rather than sudou so the deposit cannot be traced to katsuragi  ak s fee is 10 000 points for hiding his phone in the water bottle  a third party phone can be borrowed for 5 000 points to keep ak s own account clean","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p82-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3: the reward economy<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.82, 99–103<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>4 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Katsuragi offers Sudou 100,000 points for the delivery — next to Sudou&#x27;s daily 1,000–2,000.<span class=\"pg\">p.99<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> 20,000 paid as advance, 80,000 on completion, proving complicity to both parties.<span class=\"pg\">p.100<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Payment routes through AK rather than Sudou so the deposit cannot be traced to Katsuragi.<span class=\"pg\">p.101–102<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> AK&#x27;s fee is 10,000 points for hiding his phone in the water bottle.<span class=\"pg\">p.103<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p67-1\">← Before: Ch.3: summer clothes priced out<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p121-1\">→ After: Ch.4: the Outcome #1 recap<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p121-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 4  the outcome  1 recap cruise ship outcome  1  every member receives 500 000 private points and the vip 1 000 000   but the vip s class gets no class points  outcome  1 is the hardest of four  everyone in the group must identify the vip and wait until the assigned time to submit  with no early answer","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p121-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.4: the Outcome #1 recap<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.121<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p82-1\">← Before: Ch.3: the reward economy<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p154-1\">→ After: Ch.6: the reward economy<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p154-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 6  the reward economy karuizawa receives 500 000 points in september for the cruise ship vip win  special exams can pay students as much as 1 000 000 or 2 000 000 points  keeping ten to twenty percent of monthly points is ak s insurance advice   enough to erase a fatal blunder  sudou earned about 3 000 points from a club tournament  upperclassmen earn tens of thousands  club accomplishment can even affect class points","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p154-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6: the reward economy<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.154–155, 169<\/span><\/h3><details class=\"more\"><summary>1 more note<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Sudou earned about 3,000 points from a club tournament; upperclassmen earn tens of thousands.<span class=\"pg\">p.169<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p121-1\">← Before: Ch.4: the Outcome #1 recap<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p183-1\">→ After: Ch.6: lunch stakes and frugality<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p183-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 6  lunch stakes and frugality the volleyball bet is priced in private points  and ichinose s cheapness shows how class b manages its pool  the free lunch for the winning side could cost up to 10 000 points   roughly 2 000 per person for a group this size  winning would close the gap between class b and class d by that much  and losing would widen it   horikita calls it just like the special tests  ichinose says she barely spends on swimwear or fashion and will need points for something more important eventually  horikita  usually a light eater  eats freely when class b is treating","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p183-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.6: lunch stakes and frugality<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.183, 190–191<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">The volleyball bet is priced in private points, and Ichinose&#x27;s cheapness shows how Class B manages its pool.<\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Ichinose says she barely spends on swimwear or fashion and will need points for something more important eventually.<span class=\"pg\">p.190<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Horikita, usually a light eater, eats freely when Class B is treating.<span class=\"pg\">p.191<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p154-1\">← Before: Ch.6: the reward economy<\/button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p206-1\">→ After: Ch.7: the island points<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p206-1","v":"y1v4.5","y":1,"o":5,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 7  the island points the meeting opens on the class s point position  the island test reward credited to suzune has class d closing the gap  with a fresh surge of points the class is closing the gap toward the upper level classes  totals  sudou argues that someone as useless as ayanokoji shouldn t hoard the island test points","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p206-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.7: the island points<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V4.5 · p.206<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">The meeting opens on the class’s point position: the island-test reward credited to Suzune has Class D closing the gap.<\/p><details class=\"more\"><summary>2 more notes<\/summary><ul class=\"rows\"><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> With a fresh surge of points the class is closing the gap toward the upper-level classes’ totals.<span class=\"pg\">p.206<\/span><\/li><li><span class=\"mlabel\">Position<\/span> Sudou argues that someone as useless as Ayanokōji shouldn’t hoard the island-test points.<span class=\"pg\">p.206<\/span><\/li><\/ul><\/details><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v4_5-p183-1\">← Before: Ch.6: lunch stakes and frugality<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v5-p17-1","v":"y1v5","y":1,"o":6,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 2  the sports festival s point economy chabashira s handout prices the school sports festival in private points  test points and class points at once  individual competition placement pays 15 12 10 8 points on the class scoreboard  fifth place loses one point  and each place below loses one more  recommended participant events pay 50 30 15 10  fifth place loses two points  each place below loses two more  the relay pays three times those values  the winning team of a team competition takes 500 points  after combined class scores are reviewed  100 class points are deducted from the first  second  and third year batches of the two classes on the losing team  chabashira adds that points move class by class  not just by team  grade ranking is separate  50 class points to each grade s first place class  no change for second  50 deducted for third  100 for fourth  first place in an individual competition chooses 5000 private points or three written test points  second chooses 3000 or two  third chooses 1000 or one   test points accepted this way cannot be given to another student  the lowest scorer in an individual competition loses 1000 private points  or one written test point if they have fewer than 1000  overall mvp  100 000 private points  each grade s top three scorers  10 000 private points apiece  the ten lowest scoring students in each year lose ten points on their next written exam   for first years the penalty is a written exam deduction  a substitute for a recommended participant event costs 100 000 private points per competition","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v5-p17-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.2: the sports festival’s point economy<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V5 · p.17–23<\/span><\/h3><p class=\"setup\">Chabashira’s handout prices the school sports festival in private points, test points and class points at once.<\/p><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v5-p39-1\">→ After: Ch.3 sec. 3: Class D’s festival point compromise<\/button><\/div><\/article>"},{"i":"sc-private-points-y1v5-p39-1","v":"y1v5","y":1,"o":6,"k":"","w":"","q":"private points ch 3 sec  3  class d s festival point compromise horikita s compromise  carried by the class vote  top placers who don t need test score boosts spend their private points lifting the bottom placers  test scores  and the whole class shares the festival s gains and losses in private points  the narrator notes the cost   chances of victory drop across the board  and the bottom ten students per grade would still be in trouble","h":"<article class=\"card\" id=\"sc-private-points-y1v5-p39-1\"><h3><span class=\"ttl\">Ch.3 sec. 3: Class D’s festival point compromise<\/span><span class=\"meta\">Y1V5 · p.39–53<\/span><\/h3><div class=\"ptrs\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ptr\" data-go=\"sc-private-points-y1v5-p17-1\">← Before: Ch.2: the sports festival’s point economy<\/button><\/div><\/article>"}];
